absolute-path invocations bypass it. Use the bare `git` and `gh`
commands so the overlay can enforce this read allowlist (subject to its
safe-option and host checks):

- git branch, cat-file, describe, diff, grep, log, ls-files, ls-tree,
  merge-base, rev-list, rev-parse, show, status, remote, and config reads
- gh api GET reads; gh auth status; gh pr checks/diff/list/status/view;
  gh run list/view/watch; gh repo view; and gh workflow list/view

Every other invocation, including `gh issue view`, is blocked. A blocked
command always emits a `[dry-run] ... skipped` marker to stderr and returns
synthetic success (exit status 0). It also attempts to append that marker to
`<%= worktree_path %>/.babysitter-dry-run-skipped.log`; if the audit target is
unsafe or unavailable, it emits a `[dry-run] failed to write skip log ...`
warning and continues without a persistent record. Do not interpret blocked
output as a real empty result; check stderr first, then inspect the skip log
when it was written successfully.

Write your full plan to:

<%= worktree_path %>/.babysitter-dry-run-plan.md

Then exit successfully. The dry-run plan file is the contract — if it
is missing when you exit, the outcome will be reported as a failure
even when the agent exit status was zero.
